    "evidence_text": "The automated discovery of\n\nRecently word embedding techniques such as word2vec  have become very popular.\n\nOne possible solution is to use second\u2013order co\u2013occurrence vectors  ,  .\n\nshown to be successful in quantifying semantic relatedness  ,  .\n\nOur goal then is to discover methods that automatically reduce the amount of noise in a second\u2013order co\u2013occurrence vector.\n\nWe evaluate our method on two datasets that have been annotated in multiple ways.\n\nOur results show that integrating second order co\u2013occurrences with measures of semantic similarity increases correlation with our human reference standards.\n\nThese results suggest that our methods of integrating\n\nto distinguish between multiple concepts.\n\nPatwardhanP06 adapted this measure to second\u2013order co\u2013occurrence vectors.\n\nThese word vectors are averaged to create a single co-occurrence vector for the concept.\n\nThe similarity between the concepts is calculated by taking the cosine between the concepts second\u2013order vectors.\n\nMethod\tIn this section, we describe our second\u2013order similarity vector measure.\n\nThere are two stages to our approach.\n\nFirst, a co\u2013occurrence matrix must be constructed.\n\nSecond, this matrix is used to construct a second\u2013order co\u2013occurrence vector for each concept\n\nin a pair of concepts to be measured for relatedness.\n\nPrior to 1975, only 2% of the citations contained an abstract.\n\non these experiments.\n\nWe take the average of the first order co\u2013occurrence vectors associated with the terms in a super\u2013gloss\n\nThis is a second\u2013order co\u2013occurrence vector.\n\nData\tWe use two reference standards to evaluate the semantic similarity and relatedness measures .\n\nUMNSRS was annotated for both similarity and relatedness by medical residents.\n\nCorrelation between the similarity measures and human judgments were estimated using Spearman's Rank Correlation ( INLINEFORM0 ).\n\nWe used Fisher's r-to-z transformation  to calculate the significance between the correlation results.\n\nEach class of measure is followed by the scores obtained when integrating our second order vector approach with these measures of semantic similarity.\n\nWe found that INLINEFORM4 - INLINEFORM5\n\nWe found that eliminating less similar pairs improved the overall results (to a point).\n\nrelatedness.\n\nSuch embeddings could be integrated into a second\u2013order vector or be used on their own.",
